# Install {{ project_name }}
{% if kind == 'binary' %}
Read this page to run {{ project_name }} on your machine. It shows how to install a release or
build from source, and how to update.

Each [release](https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}/releases) has one archive per
platform:
{% for target in targets %}
- {{ target.name }}: `{{ project_name }}-<version>-{{ target.triple }}.{{ 'zip' if target.os == 'windows' else 'tar.gz' }}`
{%- endfor %}
{%- if 'linux' in platforms %}

The Linux programs are linked statically, so they run on any Linux distribution, Alpine included.
{%- endif %}

## Install a release

1. Download the archive for your platform and `SHA256SUMS` from the release.
{%- if visibility == 'private' %} The repository
   is private, so sign in to GitHub first, or use the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/):
   `gh release download <tag> --repo {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}`.
{%- endif %}
2. Check the download as [Verify a release](verifying-releases.md) shows.
3. Unpack the archive and move `{{ project_name }}` to where you keep programs, such as a directory
   on your `PATH`.

To update, install the new release the same way.

## Build from source

You need [Rust](https://rustup.rs/).

```sh
{%- if visibility == 'private' %}
cargo install --locked --config net.git-fetch-with-cli=true \
  --git https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }} {{ crate_name }}
{%- else %}
cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }} {{ crate_name }}
{%- endif %}
```
{%- if visibility == 'private' %}

The repository is private, so Cargo fetches it with your Git credentials.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
Read this page to run {{ project_name }} on your server. It shows how to run the container image
and how to update it.

The image is `ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}`, for {{ targets | map(attribute='name') | join(' and ') }}.
{%- if targets | length > 1 %}
Docker and Podman pull the one for your machine.
{%- endif %}
Each release pushes three tags, such as these for 1.4.2:

| Tag | Follows |
| --- | --- |
| `v1.4.2`, the version | That release only |
| `1.4`, the minor version | The latest patch release of 1.4 |
| `latest` | The latest release |
{%- if visibility == 'private' %}

## Sign in to the registry

The image is private. Create a [classic token](https://github.com/settings/tokens/new) with only
the `read:packages` scope, then sign in with it on the server:

```sh
docker login ghcr.io --username <your GitHub username> --password-stdin
```

Paste the token, press Enter, then press Ctrl-D. Podman takes the same arguments.
{%- endif %}

## Run the image

```sh
docker run -d --name {{ project_name }} --restart unless-stopped \
  ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}:<minor>
```

Replace `<minor>` with the latest release's minor version, such as `1.4`, so updates within it
arrive when you pull.

Podman takes the same arguments.
{%- if visibility == 'public' %} Check the image first as
[Verify a release](verifying-releases.md) shows.
{%- else %} To run one release exactly, use its digest, which
[Verify a release](verifying-releases.md) shows how to find.
{%- endif %}

## Update

Pull the tag again and recreate the container:

```sh
docker pull ghcr.io/{{ github_owner | lower }}/{{ project_name }}:<minor>
docker rm -f {{ project_name }}
```

Then run the image again as above. To roll back, run the previous version's tag, such as `v1.4.1`.
{%- endif %}
